{\b Chapter 2 Literature Review}\par
\par
{\b 2.1 Introduction}\par
This chapter reviews the literature supporting the study of recognition, stackability, and student trust in IT micro-credentials in Thailand. The chapter is intentionally built only from sources that are already saved locally in the thesis references folder as PDFs, downloaded HTML articles, or other directly accessible files. This makes the chapter more defensible because every citation can be checked, read, and explained. Compared with the earlier draft, this revised chapter incorporates newly obtained journal articles on employability, learner response, employer perceptions, and implementation challenges in micro-credentials. The review begins with the concept and policy relevance of micro-credentials in higher education, then examines recent empirical work, explains the theoretical foundations of signaling and legitimacy, defines the key constructs used in this thesis, and closes by identifying the specific research gap addressed by the study.\par
\par
{\b 2.2 Micro-credentials in Higher Education}\par
Micro-credentials are commonly described as shorter, more modular, and more targeted forms of learning recognition than traditional degree programs. Ahsan et al. (2023) explain that micro-credentials are usually competency-based, focus on specific skills or knowledge, and may function as an additional, alternative, complementary, or component part of a formal qualification. This modular character is important for the present thesis because it supports the idea that micro-credentials can be treated not simply as short courses, but as recognizable units that may connect to longer educational or professional pathways.\par
\par
Reed et al. (2024) strengthen this definition by describing micro-credentials as qualifications representing assessed achievement of a subset of learning at a modular level. Their discussion is particularly useful because it emphasizes that a micro-credential is meaningful only when it includes credible evidence of achievement rather than mere attendance. This point matters directly for the present study because student trust is unlikely to come from brevity alone. Trust is more likely to arise when the credential appears to represent real achievement and formal recognition.\par
\par
The broader policy literature shows why micro-credentials matter. OECD (2021) argues that the quality and value of micro-credentials depend heavily on transparency, trust, recognition, and a clear account of what exactly the credential certifies. OECD (2023) positions micro-credentials within lifelong learning and employability agendas, noting their potential to support upskilling, reskilling, and more flexible participation in education. In the Thai context, OECD (2025) highlights the need for stronger lifelong learning systems, more adaptable skills pathways, and closer alignment between learning and labor-market needs. Together, these reports make it clear that micro-credentials are not only an education technology issue. They are also linked to workforce development, institutional reform, and policy design.\par
\par
The stakeholder literature adds further nuance. Varadarajan et al. (2023) show that learners, employers, higher education institutions, and governments approach micro-credentials with different expectations. Learners tend to value short, relevant, and flexible learning; employers want clear evidence of skills and competencies; institutions care about accreditation, trust, and integration; and governments expect employability gains and wider access to lifelong learning. This is directly relevant to the present study because it suggests that students do not evaluate micro-credentials only by content. They also evaluate whether a credential seems credible, transferable, and connected to future opportunity.\par
\par
Recent empirical papers also show that the field is still stabilizing. Raj et al. (2024), in a study on professional qualifications and micro-credentials, identify major obstacles such as unclear definitions, ambiguous course descriptions, and weak accreditation and quality assurance arrangements. Their findings are important for this thesis because they show that recognition problems are not abstract. When the meaning and standing of a credential are unclear, its credibility declines. Similarly, Sharma et al. (2024) show that micro-credentials can be integrated into higher education through blended pathways or standalone online offerings, but their effectiveness depends on standardization, labor-market alignment, and strategic implementation. Ha et al. (2024) further show that employers may see clear benefits in micro-credentials while still expressing low awareness and doubt about their value. Together, these studies suggest that the key problem is no longer whether micro-credentials exist, but what makes them trusted and worthwhile.\par
\par
{\b 2.3 Emerging Empirical Evidence on Value, Employability, and Learner Response}\par
Recent empirical studies show that micro-credentials are increasingly being linked to employability and value-related outcomes. Zou et al. (2024) provide one of the strongest student-side studies in the current source set. Their results show that micro-credential learning has a positive relationship with students' perceived employability and that human capital acts as a significant mediator in that relationship. This finding is useful for the present thesis because it supports the idea that students may evaluate micro-credentials partly in terms of their future career value, not only immediate convenience.\par
\par
Sharma et al. (2024) reach a similar conclusion from a different methodological angle. Using interviews with multiple stakeholder groups, they argue that micro-credentials can augment employability when they are strategically integrated into the higher education ecosystem. However, their study also stresses that successful integration depends on enabling conditions such as standardization, delivery mode, and labor-market dynamics. In other words, employability value is not automatic. It depends on how the credential is positioned and whether stakeholders see it as meaningful.\par
\par
Tee et al. (2024) shift attention to learner-side value judgments. Their study finds that program design factors influence learning experience, and that learning experience in turn affects learner engagement and willingness to pay more for a micro-credential program. This is highly relevant to the present thesis because it shows that students respond to concrete design features rather than to the label of micro-credentials alone. Although Tee et al. (2024) focus on engagement and willingness to pay rather than trust, their findings support the broader argument that visible program characteristics can shape how learners judge worth.\par
\par
The existing student-side quantitative literature also shows a recurring tendency to frame micro-credentials through adoption or continuance models. Miao et al. (2024) study students' intention to use technology for micro-credential programs through TAM and self-determination theory, while Wang et al. (2024) examine continuance intention in MOOCs as a pathway toward micro-credentials. These studies are useful because they show what has already been done. However, they also reveal an important limitation. They focus mainly on behavioral intention, continuance, or technology use rather than on whether the credential itself appears legitimate, credible, and valuable. For the present thesis, that distinction matters. A student may be willing to use a platform or continue a course without necessarily believing that the resulting credential is trustworthy or worth investing in.\par
\par
Employer-side evidence reinforces this point. Ha et al. (2024) found that employers generally saw benefits in micro-credentials for multiple stakeholders, but they also highlighted low awareness and doubts about value as major barriers. This finding is especially important because it supports the need to study trust and recognition directly. If stakeholders doubt what a credential means, then the issue is not only participation or access. It is legitimacy.\par
\par
{\b 2.4 Theoretical Foundations}\par
\par
{\b 2.4.1 Signaling Theory}\par
Signaling theory provides the main explanatory engine for this thesis. Spence (1973) developed signaling theory to explain how people make decisions under conditions of uncertainty and information asymmetry. In such conditions, one party cannot directly observe the true quality of another and therefore relies on visible signals. In labor markets, educational credentials work as signals because they provide observable evidence that may stand in for deeper but less visible qualities.\par
\par
This logic fits micro-credentials closely. Students cannot directly know the long-term value of a micro-credential before investing time, effort, and money in it. They therefore rely on visible cues to infer value. Formal recognition suggests institutional standing. Stackability suggests future usefulness because the credential can count toward a larger pathway. Industry endorsement suggests practical relevance and labor-market connection. In other words, these features are not minor details. They are signals that help students decide whether a credential is worth trusting.\par
\par
The newly downloaded empirical literature supports this signaling perspective. Tee et al. (2024) show that learner responses are shaped by concrete program design characteristics. Zou et al. (2024) show that micro-credentials are judged partly through their perceived contribution to employability. Raj et al. (2024) show that unclear definitions, weak accreditation, and quality assurance problems undermine acceptance of micro-credentials as credible professional qualifications. Ha et al. (2024) similarly show that employers' doubts over value create barriers to effectiveness. Taken together, these studies suggest that micro-credentials are evaluated through signals of quality, recognition, and future payoff rather than through content alone.\par
\par
{\b 2.4.2 Legitimacy and Student Trust}\par
While signaling theory explains how visible cues operate under uncertainty, legitimacy theory explains why some cues are accepted as appropriate and meaningful. Deephouse et al. (2017) define legitimacy as the perceived appropriateness of an organization or practice within a broader system of norms, values, and rules. Applied to micro-credentials, legitimacy concerns whether the credential appears to fit accepted standards of higher education, assessment, and skills recognition.\par
\par
This is a useful second lens for the present study because micro-credentials do not gain value only by existing. They gain value when students believe they are institutionally appropriate, officially meaningful, and socially recognized. OECD (2021) repeatedly emphasizes trust, transparency, and recognition as central issues in micro-credential ecosystems. Reed et al. (2024) show that higher education faculty focus heavily on assessment quality and evidence of achievement when designing micro-credentials. Raj et al. (2024) show that lack of accreditation and quality assurance weakens credibility. Ha et al. (2024) show that low employer awareness and doubts over value remain practical barriers. Sharma et al. (2024) add that standardization and strategic integration are necessary if micro-credentials are to support employability effectively. These studies all point in the same direction: legitimacy is not automatic, and trust must be earned.\par
\par
In this thesis, student trust is treated as the student-level expression of these broader legitimacy judgments. Students may not explicitly use terms such as legitimacy or institutional appropriateness, but they still form practical judgments about whether a credential seems credible, dependable, and worth believing in. Ohanian (1990) is useful at this point because it provides a classic validated source-credibility scale built around trustworthiness, expertise, and attractiveness. Although that study comes from advertising rather than higher education, it is still valuable for the present thesis because it shows how credibility judgments can be operationalized through trustworthiness and expertise cues. For this reason, Ohanian (1990) strengthens the measurement logic of student trust in the present study, while the legitimacy literature explains why those judgments matter. Recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement are therefore expected to matter because they reduce uncertainty and make the credential appear more institutionally grounded.\par
\par
{\b 2.5 Key Constructs in the Present Study}\par
Based on the literature reviewed above, the present study focuses on three external signals and two internal judgments.\par
\par
Recognition refers to whether the micro-credential is formally acknowledged by a university or another recognized educational structure. This construct is strongly supported by OECD (2021), Varadarajan et al. (2023), Raj et al. (2024), and Ha et al. (2024), all of which show that value depends partly on whether stakeholders understand the credential and see it as legitimate.\par
\par
Stackability refers to whether the micro-credential can accumulate into future academic credit or a larger qualification pathway. This construct is grounded in the modular character of micro-credentials discussed by Ahsan et al. (2023) and Reed et al. (2024), and in the pathways discussion in OECD (2023). Sharma et al. (2024) also support this logic by showing that micro-credentials can be integrated into broader higher education ecosystems rather than remaining isolated credentials.\par
\par
Industry endorsement refers to visible connection with employers, industry bodies, or professional partners. This construct is important because it signals that the credential has relevance outside the classroom. Ha et al. (2024) provide direct support by examining employers' perceptions of micro-credentials, while Sharma et al. (2024) and Zou et al. (2024) reinforce the broader employability logic that makes external relevance important to students.\par
\par
Student trust refers to the extent to which the credential appears credible, dependable, and institutionally meaningful. In the present thesis, trust is not treated as a general personality trait. It is a judgment about the credential itself. Deephouse et al. (2017), OECD (2021), Reed et al. (2024), Raj et al. (2024), and Ha et al. (2024) all support the idea that quality assurance, recognition, and appropriate design shape whether educational offerings are believed.\par
\par
Perceived value refers to the student's overall judgment that the credential is worthwhile relative to the time, effort, and other sacrifices required. Alves (2010) is helpful here because it presents a higher education unidimensional approach in which perceived value can be measured robustly through the trade-off between price and quality together with comparison against alternatives. Amado-Mateus et al. (2023) then provide the strongest accessible recent measurement-oriented source for this construct in higher education by evaluating the psychometric properties of a university perceived-value scale. Together, these two sources make the construct more defensible for the present thesis because they show both the conceptual logic of perceived value in higher education and a recent accessible validation route. This allows perceived value to be treated as something broader than simple interest or intention. Tee et al. (2024) support this broader interpretation by showing that learner judgments of experience connect to engagement and willingness to pay, while Zou et al. (2024) reinforce the importance of expected career outcomes.\par
\par
{\b 2.6 Previous Models, Measurement Direction, and Research Gap}\par
The accessible literature suggests that existing empirical work around micro-credentials falls into several broad groups. One group examines implementation, design, and stakeholder issues at a system level (Ahsan et al., 2023; Varadarajan et al., 2023; Reed et al., 2024; Raj et al., 2024; Ha et al., 2024; Sharma et al., 2024). A second group examines learner-side behavioral outcomes such as engagement, willingness to pay, intention, and continuance (Tee et al., 2024; Miao et al., 2024; Wang et al., 2024). A third group examines employability and future career-related outcomes (Zou et al., 2024; Sharma et al., 2024).\par
\par
This literature is valuable, but it still leaves a clear gap. First, much of the quantitative student-side work remains framed in terms of adoption, engagement, or continuance rather than legitimacy and trust. Second, the literature contains useful discussion of recognition, accreditation, design quality, and stakeholder expectations, but these factors are rarely combined into a single student-centered explanatory model. Third, the available studies are not focused on Thai IT students, even though OECD (2025) highlights the growing importance of skills flexibility and lifelong learning in Thailand. Fourth, much of the existing survey work asks respondents for general perceptions of programs they already know, rather than testing how specific credential features shape judgments.\par
\par
These gaps justify the present thesis. The current study brings together recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement as visible signals; student trust as the mediating judgment; and perceived value as the primary outcome. This design moves beyond a basic adoption model by asking not simply whether students intend to use micro-credentials, but what makes a credential appear credible and worth pursuing in the first place. The choice of perceived value as the main outcome is also more consistent with the present research problem because students may be interested in a credential without believing it has real educational or labor-market value.\par
\par
The measurement direction of the thesis also follows logically from the literature. Alves (2010) supports a higher education view of perceived value as a trade-off judgment, while Amado-Mateus et al. (2023) provide an accessible basis for perceived value items in higher education. Ohanian (1990) strengthens the logic for trust-related measurement by offering a validated credibility scale built around trustworthiness and expertise judgments. The newly available student survey papers, especially Tee et al. (2024), Miao et al. (2024), Wang et al. (2024), and Zou et al. (2024), further show that adapted Likert-scale instruments remain common and defensible in this field. However, the present thesis differs from those studies by centering the micro-credential itself as the object of judgment and by testing concrete design signals through a survey-experimental approach.\par
\par
{\b 2.7 Chapter Summary}\par
This chapter reviewed the literature supporting the study from a strictly defensible source set. The literature shows that micro-credentials are modular and skills-focused credentials whose value depends on assessment, transparency, recognition, and stakeholder confidence. Recent studies also show that micro-credentials are increasingly linked to employability, willingness to invest, and broader learning pathways, but they also face credibility challenges when definitions, accreditation, standardization, and recognition are weak. Signaling theory explains why visible features such as recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement matter under uncertainty. Legitimacy theory explains why those features shape whether the credential appears appropriate and believable. Perceived value research supports the use of value as a stronger outcome than simple adoption intention. Together, these strands justify a model in which recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement shape student trust, and student trust shapes perceived value among Thai IT students. The next chapter presents the conceptual framework and hypotheses derived from this review.\par
